  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-03-17T04:19:24.698Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I have a Commodore 64 world with a power button on the monitor and a C= key on the keyboard you can click, and I've had reports that desktop users can't click the power button. \nThe only difference between the two triggers is that the power button is fully inside the colliders of the monitor it's attached to, while the C= key sticks out a bit from its colliders. \nVR users can interact with both of these triggers just fine, but desktop users can't click the power button, so it led to my launching the world thinking it was in full working order when it was not. The expected behavior is that anything a VR user can interact with a dektop user should be able to as well, so that's why I've reported this as a bug. I don't know if the different lies in how the targeting lasers work, or if the reason VR users can interact with the triggers is because they can technically put their hands inside the wall, but if its the latter then perhaps desktop users should have an interaction collider which is at arms length and can also go inside walls?\nObviously I can easily fix this in my world by moving the trigger collider out a bit, but people will continue to accidentally make worlds that aren't desktop friendly if the interaction system isn't consistent in this way.",
